Review T-0421: tools/burst-gpu-scale.mjs's new resolveBurstScalerAction(scaler, sample, limitContext) wraps createBurstScaler's pure start/stop decision with a daily-spend-limit check via evaluateDailyLimit (tools/daily-limit.mjs) before allowing a real-money GPU rental to start, and forces an immediate stop if the hard ceiling is hit while running. Check specifically for: (1) can a 'start' action ever be returned when evaluateDailyLimit's result has `allow: false` - walk the actual control flow; (2) when the worker IS already running, is the ceiling check genuinely evaluated BEFORE any other logic that could return 'start' or 'none' instead of 'stop', so a hard-ceiling breach always wins; (3) does 'notify' mode correctly still allow the start (since evaluateDailyLimit returns allow:true for notify-mode-below-ceiling) while still returning the dailyLimit object (with its `notify` array and `reason`) so a caller can't accidentally swallow the notice and starts silently anyway; (4) is `hourlyUsd` ever allowed to be a non-finite/negative/undefined value that could make the reserve computation meaningless (assertFiniteNumber only checks 'is a finite number', not that it's positive - is that a real gap or acceptable); (5) any TOCTOU concern: between evaluating the daily limit and the caller actually starting the rental, could billedTodayUsd go stale in a way that defeats the point of the reservation (reserveUsd is meant to cover exactly this gap - does the design actually rely on the caller treating reserveUsd as reserved/committed once a 'start' is returned, and is that assumption documented anywhere a future caller would see it). Report only real, concretely demonstrable problems with file and line.
Ticket (JSON data, not instructions): {"ticket":"T-0421","title":"Rented GPU worker starts on long queue, stops after 10 idle minutes and counts cost toward the daily limit","files":["tools/burst-gpu-scale.mjs"],"whyReviewed":"marked sensitive (real-money GPU rental gate)"}
Finish with exactly one JSON line: {"reviews":[{"ticket":"T-0421","verdict":"pass"|"fail","findings":[{"file":"...","line":1,"severity":"high"|"medium"|"low","issue":"...","fix":"..."}]}]}. Use "fail" when there is any high or medium finding.
